##
    Versions of a name that a call cannot choose between.

    This program does not compile. Every mistake here is a call rather than a declaration: the
    versions below are all perfectly good functions, and each of them would be reachable on its
    own. What fails is asking for one of them in a way that does not settle which.

    The working side of the same rules is in samples/overloads.pc.
##

# Nothing here is run, so nothing reads what it declares and nothing keeps what it
# works out. That is the shape of every negative, and not the lesson of this one.
# ignore PC0410 in file

model Animal
    # @summary: What this animal says.
    # @yields: nothing in particular.
    public virtual string function Speaks()
        yield "...";
    end function
end model

shared model Program

    function Main()
        ##
            Both versions accept an integer, and neither is nearer than the other: an integer
            widens to a real and to a fraction, and nothing in the language says which of those
            it would rather be. This is the tie the compiler refuses to break, because breaking
            it would mean the answer depended on a rule nobody remembers — or worse, on which
            version happened to be written first.
        ##
        Console.WriteLine(Program.Wide(1));

        # No version takes a boolean, and neither string nor integer accepts one.
        Console.WriteLine(Program.Only(true));

        # The count is asked before anything else, so this is not even a near miss.
        Console.WriteLine(Program.Only(1, 2));

        ##
            A fraction reaches a real only when asked, because thirds have no exact decimal
            form. So the version taking a real does not accept one, and there is no other.
        ##
        Console.WriteLine(Program.Measure(1|3));

        ##
            The lambda fits both: it is a function of that shape, and every function is a
            Function. Neither version is nearer, so this is a tie like the first one — and the
            way out is to say which by naming the type, rather than to add a rule about roots.
        ##
        Console.WriteLine(Program.Held((n) yield n + 1));

        ##
            Boxing does not exist, so an integer is not a Model and no widening reaches one.
            This is a plain mismatch rather than a tie: there is one version and it says no.
        ##
        Console.WriteLine(Program.Boxed(1));

        # An Animal is not a Dog, and a version taking the child does not accept the parent.
        Console.WriteLine(Program.Walk(new Animal()));
    end function

    string function Wide(real r)
        yield "real";
    end function

    string function Wide(fraction f)
        yield "fraction";
    end function

    string function Only(integer n)
        yield "integer";
    end function

    string function Only(string s)
        yield "string";
    end function

    string function Measure(real r)
        yield "real";
    end function

    string function Held(integer delegate(integer) f)
        yield "shaped";
    end function

    string function Held(Function f)
        yield "any";
    end function

    string function Boxed(Model m)
        yield "model";
    end function

    string function Walk(Dog d)
        yield "dog";
    end function

end model

# @summary: An animal that says something in particular.
model Dog extends Animal
    # @summary: What a dog says.
    # @yields: "woof".
    public override string function Speaks()
        yield "woof";
    end function
end model
